      "source_example_id": "e-8605",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Map Relative to Two Bases Given One Basis",
      "kp_id": "e-8605"
    },
    "q-140435": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140435",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 & 3 & 6 & 18 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -9 & -3 & 27\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -9 & -3 & 27\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -12 & -9 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & -4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 & 12 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-140435-a",
