    "q-97544": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97544",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -4 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -4 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
